{"full":"microsoft/T-MAC","name":"T-MAC","description":"Low-bit LLM inference on CPU/NPU with lookup table","category":"Other","language":"C++","stars":994,"forks":90,"created":"2024-02-02T05:14:07Z","updated":"2025-06-05T12:26:45Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"","topics":[],"demo":null,"color":"#302e55","ink":"#dbd2ff","availability":"available","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-04T05:48:20.309Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-04T05:48:20.309Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-04T05:48:20.309Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T08:25:05.722Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T21:18:00.752Z","stars":993,"forks":90},{"at":"2026-10-04T05:48:20.309Z","stars":994,"forks":90}],"technologies":[],"overviewAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T22:14:43.218Z","overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["T-MAC is a kernel library to directly support mixed-precision matrix multiplication (int1/2/3/4 x int8/fp16/fp32) without the need for dequantization by utilizing lookup tables. T-MAC aims to boost low-bit LLM inference on CPUs. T-MAC already offers support for various low-bit models, including W4A16 from GPTQ/gguf, W2A16 from BitDistiller/EfficientQAT and W1(.58)A8 from BitNet on OSX/Linux/Windows equipped with ARM/Intel CPUs.","T-MAC achieves a token generation throughput of 20 tokens/sec with a single core and 48 tokens/sec with four cores on Surface Laptop 7 for 3B BitNet, which is a 45x speedup compared to SOTA CPU low-bit framework (llama.cpp). T-MAC can even reach 11 tokens/sec on lower-end devices like Raspberry Pi 5."],"features":["05/28/2025 🚀🚀: The idea of T-MAC extends its capabilities to NPU! For more information, check out the t-man README and try BitNet/Qwen3/Llama3 with the demo app!","10/21/2024 🎉🎉: BitNet, powered by T-MAC, is open-sourced.","10/10/2024 🚀🚀: By updating and rebasing our llama.cpp version, T-MAC now support more models (e.g., qwen2) and the end-to-end performance is further improved by 1015%! Try qwen2 using the Official GPTQ model.","08/21/2024 🎉🎉: T-MAC paper is accepted by EuroSys 2025.","08/17/2024 🚀: T-MAC now supports 1/2/4-bit quantized models of (almost) any architecture in GPTQ format."],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/microsoft/T-MAC/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"ae3d63264c0a8a4fe87195db5c61780bad3b6ff85b262c6d3d0aed7007185ffb","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T07:02:41.704Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T22:14:46.615Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"T-MAC is a kernel library to directly support mixed-precision matrix multiplication (int1/2/3/4 x int8/fp16/fp32) without the need for dequantization by utilizing lookup tables. T-MAC aims to boost low-bit LLM inference on CPUs. T-MAC already offers support for various low-bit models, including W4A16 from GPTQ/gguf, W2A16 from BitDistiller/EfficientQAT and W1(.58)A8 from BitNet on OSX/Linux/Windows equipped with ARM/Intel CPUs.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/microsoft/T-MAC/blob/main/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"ae3d63264c0a8a4fe87195db5c61780bad3b6ff85b262c6d3d0aed7007185ffb","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/microsoft/T-MAC/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-03T22:14:46.616Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2025-06-05T12:26:45Z","enrichmentAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T10:41:07.036Z","latestRelease":{"name":"T-MAC 1.0.0a5 Release","tag":"1.0.0a5","publishedAt":"2025-05-27T19:08:17Z","url":"https://github.com/microsoft/T-MAC/releases/tag/1.0.0a5"},"releaseCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T10:41:07.036Z","screenshots":[],"readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/microsoft/T-MAC/blob/main/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-03T22:14:46.618Z","hash":"ae3d63264c0a8a4fe87195db5c61780bad3b6ff85b262c6d3d0aed7007185ffb","originalBytes":21903,"storedBytes":21903,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["t-mac","build","llama.cpp","install","cmake","cpu","running","speedup","users","performance","tvm","user","llama-2-7b","jianyu","models","tokens","bit","conda","model","powershell","rdparty","clang","command","compar